WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.799
How do you build RRI into
a real research project?

2
00:00:04.079 --> 00:00:08.602
Here are six practical steps, from framing
your question to sharing your results.

3
00:00:09.802 --> 00:00:12.597
First, frame the research
question responsibly.

4
00:00:12.877 --> 00:00:15.501
Ask: what problem are we
solving, and for whom?

5
00:00:15.781 --> 00:00:21.508
Make sure the question reflects real societal
needs, not just academic curiosity or

6
00:00:21.508 --> 00:00:22.543
market drivers.

7
00:00:23.743 --> 00:00:29.107
Second, map your stakeholders, and plan
for real, ongoing engagement, rather than a

8
00:00:29.107 --> 00:00:30.335
token consultation.

9
00:00:31.535 --> 00:00:34.927
A helpful tool is the influence
versus interest matrix.

10
00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:38.663
Powerful and highly interested
groups should be close partners.

11
00:00:38.943 --> 00:00:44.149
Influential but less engaged actors, such
as regulators, should be kept satisfied.

12
00:00:45.349 --> 00:00:47.055
But RRI adds a twist.

13
00:00:47.335 --> 00:00:52.039
Pay special attention to people with a high
stake and little power, such as affected

14
00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:52.711
communities.

15
00:00:52.991 --> 00:00:57.599
Lower the barriers so that their voices
are heard, through interviews, workshops,

16
00:00:57.599 --> 00:00:59.135
surveys or advisory groups.

17
00:01:00.335 --> 00:01:02.063
Third, anticipate impacts.

18
00:01:02.343 --> 00:01:07.099
A futures wheel places your innovation in
the centre, adds its direct effects, and

19
00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:09.767
then asks what each of
those effects leads to.

20
00:01:10.047 --> 00:01:12.714
Hidden harms often appear
at this second level.

21
00:01:13.914 --> 00:01:16.047
Keep three questions in front of the team.

22
00:01:16.327 --> 00:01:17.394
What could go wrong?

23
00:01:17.674 --> 00:01:18.783
Who could be harmed?

24
00:01:19.063 --> 00:01:20.663
And what dilemmas could emerge?

25
00:01:21.863 --> 00:01:23.762
Fourth, embed reflexivity.

26
00:01:24.042 --> 00:01:28.543
Build time into the project to question
your assumptions and your positionality.

27
00:01:28.823 --> 00:01:33.334
Many teams keep a researcher journal,
to track ethical reflections and key

28
00:01:33.334 --> 00:01:33.943
decisions.

29
00:01:35.143 --> 00:01:37.170
Fifth, design for responsiveness.

30
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:41.755
Define checkpoints and triggers in
advance, so you know what would make you

31
00:01:41.755 --> 00:01:45.429
re-evaluate, who decides, and how
the change will be documented.

32
00:01:45.709 --> 00:01:50.734
For example, you might change your data
collection strategy if a community expresses

33
00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:51.213
concern.

34
00:01:52.413 --> 00:01:57.575
Finally, plan for open and inclusive
dissemination, beyond academic journals.

35
00:01:57.855 --> 00:01:59.242
Then run this quick check.

36
00:01:59.522 --> 00:02:01.463
Have you involved key stakeholders?

37
00:02:01.743 --> 00:02:03.770
Explored impacts beyond your field?

38
00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.821
Made your assumptions transparent?

39
00:02:06.101 --> 00:02:07.445
Can your design adapt?

40
00:02:07.725 --> 00:02:10.413
And will your results
reach, and benefit, society?

41
00:02:11.613 --> 00:02:12.957
Thank you for watching.

42
00:02:13.237 --> 00:02:16.757
Continue with the module to
put these ideas into practice.
